    "product_id": "cbb470e5-d250-46c3-8526-f6d6d00ce051",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 010640\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1140 PM PDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Relatively drier and cooler conditions expected through the work\n  week, though uncertainty increases this weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Monday.\n\nSouthwesterly flow will maintain dry conditions through most of the \nweek. Moisture from Hurricane Karina may get pulled into this flow \npattern, but forecast soundings indicate most of this moisture \nshould stay aloft, resulting in mid and high cloud cover rather than \nprecipitation. The cloud cover will help keep temperatures around \nfive degrees below normal for late September from Wednesday through \nFriday. Elevated winds at 850 mb will also support gusty \nsouthwesterly winds each afternoon most days. Forecast confidence \ndecreases heading into the weekend. There is a tropical disturbance \nlocated several hundred miles off the west coast of Mexico that has \na 90 percent chance of cyclone formation over the next seven days. \nHigh amounts of forecast uncertainty remains as models seem to be \ngravitating towards two different solutions. One solution takes the \nsystem west into the Pacific Ocean, which would have little effect \non weather in the forecast area besides increased cloud cover. The \nother solution tracks north towards the California Coast. Should \nthis solution prevail, widespread precipitation is within the realm \nof possibility. Models have slowed down the progression of the \nsystem in this solution, with precipitation chances lasting into \nMonday should it occur. At this time, the ECMWF Ensemble has most \nmembers indicating the westerly track, while the GEFS Ensemble has a \nwider range of outcomes, with members split between the solutions. \nAgain, plenty of uncertainty at this time and will continue to \nmonitor trends with upcoming forecasts. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...South-\nsoutheast outflow from earlier, distant storms should continue for \nanother few hours based on current trends, finally weakening late \ntonight. For the rest of the TAF period, south-southwest winds are \nexpected, strongest from late morning through sunset with gusts 20-\n30 knots. Few to scattered clouds around 12kft, with mostly clear \nskies late tonight and tomorrow. Temps remain below 100F.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Convection has largely \ndissipated across eastern Lincoln and northern Mohave counties this \nevening, allowing dry conditions to prevail overnight. Isolated \nshowers/storms possible in far northeasterly Mohave County Tuesday \nafternoon, but coverage and intensity will be notably less than \nMonday. Elsewhere, breezy southerly to westerly winds persist with \ngusts generally 15-30 knots. Strongest winds from late morning \nthrough sunset in most locations. Few to scattered clouds at or \nabove 10kft in far eastern portions of the area, with clear skies to \nthe west.\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Meltzer\nAVIATION...Woods\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
